er.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ba268ed-b53f-5f86-afdf-a086184c580d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396374} :answer-field/id #uuid "869f87d8-d462-5ef0-919e-8c5fd622fb0b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396372} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab5d3c4f-33de-5eef-8ae7-7817054b32b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396379} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb05416-e870-500e-9a56-00e679e530ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbb05416-e870-500e-9a56-00e679e530ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396380} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd4e31a3-2c33-5488-8129-ccd3b7edb5c9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396378} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168810da-b39a-596e-97d5-9d3f2cee753e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396382} {:answer/id #uuid "5191241f-ad91-527c-9c7d-fd7612a78fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396383} {:answer/id #uuid "8e35eb45-ae11-5d49-8ed6-d440fcdf4c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396384} {:answer/id #uuid "047e1746-629c-5d4a-bc0b-01f3e0f827c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396385} {:answer/id #uuid "3beef058-c56a-5a74-a72d-b0d8e2526d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3beef058-c56a-5a74-a72d-b0d8e2526d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396386} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2436d9b-713f-53b8-9755-f99ff60b4717" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "189a55f5-72ee-590d-b213-1f6bb96fbc77" :question/math-academy-id "q-334685" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3{e}^{2x}+2x,$ then $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below\n\n $\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2x}{3{e}^{2x}}\\right)=0$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,2x=O(3{e}^{2x})$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x})$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O({e}^{2x})$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O(\\underbrace{3{e}^{2x}+2x}_{f})\n$$\n\nand this is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3},$ we have\n\n$$\n2x=O(3{e}^{2x}).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the $\\text{absorption property},$ we have \n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)=O(3{e}^{2x}).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x})\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O({e}^{2x}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb43023-36ff-554c-9bd6-3c3eb9fd2b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308964} {:answer/id #uuid "645537b4-3813-58a4-830f-5035f8d6f848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309519} {:answer/id #uuid "929439de-a37e-5a8f-9451-f6aa929c7c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309918} {:answer/id #uuid "b90ce372-56d3-5a38-a137-81c87762446a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "929439de-a37e-5a8f-9451-f6aa929c7c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309918} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a4f1021-b4d9-56f7-ad14-ad0a229b0d96" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310823} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa728d3-f5b6-56d2-9ec3-1289237b9939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308957} {:answer/id #uuid "5615fc26-ee71-5bc0-8c95-b2d425d1ec12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309396} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7dccbd-c0c4-5777-b569-75b9e81cf024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309836} {:answer/id #uuid "e23c7789-e34b-53e8-97ce-249f0d492897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a7dccbd-c0c4-5777-b569-75b9e81cf024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309836} :answer-field/id #uuid "38e68cae-4e6e-5e48-8c73-fd484fcdfb2a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310945} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0984ea51-e01d-5736-8e8b-d6636428a5c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308816} {:answer/id #uuid "4cbaa83c-b11d-52ac-9781-706a9bbc4e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309331} {:answer/id #uuid "90f2e29a-30cc-5474-937f-00a43202db18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309906} {:answer/id #uuid "a28bb804-62ff-516e-a9af-98c7e6d7b311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310059} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c150a2-d371-5ff5-a5ae-de296c22168a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a28bb804-62ff-516e-a9af-98c7e6d7b311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310059} :answer-field/id #uuid "e470efd5-da8b-5cdf-990c-29380d19556b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89999e08-d505-549a-bd78-727ab6426321" :question/math-academy-id "q-337179" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =4{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =2+2{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =2+2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2+2{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9ab5f9-e0df-5f65-a021-59f32c7c7165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=2t,\\,y-6={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486650} {:answer/id #uuid "50ed4773-3ed9-5b89-9333-b39bf928515e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1={t}^{2},\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486772} {:answer/id #uuid "54fff804-525b-5500-af11-d8004b02fdf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486779} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb30a1c-48e6-50f2-b4fa-5876780a57ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487010} {:answer/id #uuid "f03d06b8-4245-5331-81e6-9913e1da8421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-t,\\,y+6=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54fff804-525b-5500-af11-d8004b02fdf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486779} :answer-field/id #uuid "791d2dca-a053-5eb5-a884-1ab282013d73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e847f0a2-135f-5fe9-88a5-7da062fb29a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-84459" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-2)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,6)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,6),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4 &=2pt \\\\ 8 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{\\left(-\\frac{2}{p}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)t,\\,y-(-2)=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bcdaab6-34bc-5cf0-920a-1920fb37788d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352521} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf23584-4cc5-5a62-b7de-fb2e55c64458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352909} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9ef262-bb28-5e12-ad5e-ab3f87650177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353173} {:answer/id #uuid "e3106343-e7c0-5b6b-8a50-8d6ac691410c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=\\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353542} {:answer/id #uuid "ff638889-0eb9-5f29-bb21-b3200514aa48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=\\frac{5}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc9ef262-bb28-5e12-ad5e-ab3f87650177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353173} :answer-field/id #uuid "abaff4b2-cd37-5ce8-930c-1cb1903f7b7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9821351-2cd3-5e5a-a4b5-1b6135a99522" :question/math-academy-id "q-84462" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-1)^{2}=-5(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-1=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p(x-1) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p & =-5 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39529e1e-2088-505f-9e34-bbc1c3be2f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=2t,\\,y-2=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486715} {:answer/id #uuid "471851da-2777-566c-aca4-80a6129293ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=-4{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486748} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7a6075-a431-53ee-bc18-5eceb959098b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-4t,\\,y-4=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486868} {:answer/id #uuid "b8700ae7-73f0-5f02-8801-a5a0722e1c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=4t,\\,y-4=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486998} {:answer/id #uuid "e149d119-0167-5e62-a13f-d6a2d2aeb067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7a6075-a431-53ee-bc18-5eceb959098b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=-4t,\\,y-4=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486868} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc264f7f-ffc3-5bf3-a557-71a3989333ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6266b55e-c4d6-52a5-b8a7-d0b0754b47be" :question/math-academy-id "q-84464" :question/problem "![](images/34/34e9beebbecc3eff77faa9b01f4684e334ec7cbdcbd9b00e328f95f7be88c1d4.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-2,4),$ and passes through the point $(2,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=2pt,\\,y-4=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(2,2),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=2$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2pt \\\\ -2 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{\\left(\\frac{2}{p}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=2(-2)t,\\,y-4=(-2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=-4t,\\,y-4=-2{t}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302d353b-a56f-5c24-9f51-d27538caecd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-3{t}^{2},\\,y+5=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352006} {:answer/id #uuid "579d5d7a-937a-5e4b-b254-12477d1f60d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=6t,\\,y+1=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352340} {:answer/id #uuid "83531b39-1632-5a9b-b982-06d44c0ac31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=6{t}^{2},\\,y-1=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352715} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e7406e-1f65-58f8-b3b4-5ea6927da113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353143} {:answer/id #uuid "bd29111e-fe20-58b0-b9d5-282ba37d292e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=3t,\\,y-5=6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7e7406e-1f65-58f8-b3b4-5ea6927da113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353143} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4358c3-f15f-501b-ba43-cb878c186919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "367eead1-51e7-5f05-9cdc-3a8e5cc1c6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-84467" :question/problem "![](images/75/75df54dd30da590814e89153396ac7cf8a713fc9a6e04d14786780147ba57cfe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(5,-1),$ and passes through the point $(17,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(5,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(17,11),$ we can substitute $x=17$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{\\left(\\frac{6}{p}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{36}{12} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(3){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23911304-8fc2-5561-9525-01cac991662b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=24(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351888} {:answer/id #uuid "313ce0ec-4570-5871-b5c0-e74125caa971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352024} {:answer/id #uuid "7047a78d-9858-5e6a-9ecf-c0bd156ab4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=6(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352553} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3e7980-d4d2-5567-9f8e-474402dba20f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=6(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353046} {:answer/id #uuid "f82a350e-4f08-57f2-a427-9a9dad7ea665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae3e7980-d4d2-5567-9f8e-474402dba20f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=6(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353046} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3a291d-30fa-5a7d-9b82-7059959aa63b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b1571ae-cba4-599c-b064-df002d19a987" :question/math-academy-id "q-84469" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-6=3t,\\,y-3=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-6}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{x-6}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y-3)}{3} \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}\\right) \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =6(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047f7c89-95e8-5593-9db9-903b49399668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=6(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351610} {:answer/id #uuid "3f63effc-63e8-5acf-8dbc-9d91d6ec3f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352154} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9fb08b-d965-5537-9311-17fe0d6e24ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=3(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353021} {:answer/id #uuid "c04e2ca2-5146-5422-831b-6787c6e1cf6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353224} {:answer/id #uuid "d0616c6a-e627-504d-8c3b-c6f54a495b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=6(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047f7c89-95e8-5593-9db9-903b49399668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=6(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351610} :answer-field/id #uuid "4626bb40-b447-5a39-baa8-cb737d50bf57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e4599b9-0663-5b78-a942-16f34dae42fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-84850" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=3t,\\,y+2=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-2}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{x-2}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y+2)}{3} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}\\right) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =6(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07843e2a-4ff0-5606-88dd-fb6df01f2a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548371} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee6e0f1-6d16-57f9-853b-709deedcda1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548597} {:answer/id #uuid "99017c36-c974-535a-a6b7-1a935211faff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548657} {:answer/id #uuid "acfe2f60-036e-554b-a924-88f87430de8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548696} {:answer/id #uuid "faa1a3c0-c86e-5107-b638-b2b6168befce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99017c36-c974-535a-a6b7-1a935211faff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548657} :answer-field/id #uuid "83f053be-9090-5811-8315-530372e873fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b7ad171-3d97-5b1b-b8d8-95af987a1b58" :question/math-academy-id "q-87581" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3{e}^{x}-4<0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{x}-4 & <0 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & <4 \\\\ {e}^{x} & <\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ x & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8449e518-3201-532e-92e4-f195d11fd40b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\le \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 5 - \\ln 7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156304} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c56fa6-5b7a-5033-9c72-75503f5df0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\le \\frac{\\ln 5}{\\ln 2 - \\ln 7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156305} {:answer/id #uuid "eed9696d-22cd-57c6-8352-560e7cbbe479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\ge \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 5 + \\ln 7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156306} {:answer/id #uuid "3317af7c-fc39-578b-b7c2-b601914bf4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\ge \\frac{\\ln 5}{\\ln 2 - \\ln 7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156307} {:answer/id #uuid "77af8b58-7172-5659-92d1-7b09c7c8cbdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\le \\frac{\\ln 7}{\\ln 5 - \\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156308}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5794ac5b-3648-5f2d-aadc-1085dcc00684" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156303}] :question/id #uuid "7d589296-d5a8-5896-a624-c1dd72074d92" :question/math-academy-id "q-87583" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)^{x} + 1 \\ge 16$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c2f01c-8615-576b-afee-a4d3e9a06fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354844} {:answer/id #uuid "394af28e-4f29-540d-b831-776d48393666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355181} {:answer/id #uuid "9c03be19-be56-5b1d-9f9e-d8fbf571ad8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356047} {:answer/id #uuid "a4556757-f058-59ff-ae28-248014efa357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356123} {:answer/id #uuid "c44ee22c-ab3d-56c5-8166-977968b73a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4556757-f058-59ff-ae28-248014efa357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356123} :answer-field/id #uuid "9effbdb3-cd9d-543f-bbd0-0059f077707d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0658c406-65bc-5e75-acc3-6aa4bce27134" :question/math-academy-id "q-89424" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y+{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y+{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2(2y+{x}^{2})+2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4y+2{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,-1)} & ={(4y+2{x}^{2}+2x)|}_{(1,-1)} \\\\  & =4(-1)+2(1)^{2}+2(1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "959338a7-60c4-5a55-9f1b-a865446e2d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448768} {:answer/id #uuid "69b15ebd-c02c-5646-ae02-ba78235b5764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69b15ebd-c02c-5646-ae02-ba78235b5764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448769} :answer-field/id #uuid "96297067-e385-57a2-a3ae-fd6531ec5be0" :answer-field/key "$(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449424} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6c32f79-d6e6-52aa-8abc-ebccac7a5f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448770} {:answer/id #uuid "5a94377a-b103-57b7-9ad0-02e8de056f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6c32f79-d6e6-52aa-8abc-ebccac7a5f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448770} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d38ca81-a578-55c5-b6d4-b75a5b0b47b3" :answer-field/key "$(1,2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "688c8cdf-d80b-5a35-b096-3aebfa3b9f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448772} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1f02b0-5a61-5e05-aa2f-f35e35902339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "688c8cdf-d80b-5a35-b096-3aebfa3b9f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448772} :answer-field/id #uuid "67de780c-c66d-5d8e-8494-1c4059c8a87d" :answer-field/key "$(1,2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71ecf34a-b807-5183-a2fd-953c773f847a" :question/math-academy-id "q-89428" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{3}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,2),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(2{x}^{3}-y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =2(1)^{3}-(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =6(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3}+(2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=0,\n$$\n\n we obtain that $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement “$(1,2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$” is false. There is a critical point at $(1,2).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)}>0,\n$$\n\n we have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement “$(1,2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$” is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8268ccf0-99a1-5d50-8124-3a25abf40250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460371} {:answer/id #uuid "467412e3-15b3-512a-a802-f684b3aad556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8268ccf0-99a1-5d50-8124-3a25abf40250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460371} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f12e9d4-9113-5647-8742-cb9eb7878c4c" :answer-field/key "$(0,e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5834a2e-2485-5518-9c4e-a231b610596c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460373} {:answer/id #uuid "36f7dc46-36f6-5ffb-8b98-9f2a040465ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5834a2e-2485-5518-9c4e-a231b610596c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460373} :answer-field/id #uuid "a632297b-aee6-55bd-ae1d-e83e983aa846" :answer-field/key "$(0,e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461053} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f85fb627-473f-5914-840c-ec1363bd899e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460375} {:answer/id #uuid "29a59bd9-65df-5364-b6dd-86ae7f65047c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f85fb627-473f-5914-840c-ec1363bd899e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460375} :answer-field/id #uuid "203247ac-2cf9-5872-b932-97a101cdfd65" :answer-field/key "$(0,e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ad3120d-6b06-5341-b227-fa4c2d83974e" :question/math-academy-id "q-89891" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,e)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,e),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {e}^{x}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(y{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}+e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$(0,e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)}=e\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,e)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement “$(0,e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$” is false. There is no critical point at $(0,e),$ therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement “$(0,e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$” is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,e).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161671 :question/id #uuid "0e42cf0f-f1bd-5762-a4fc-3cedec697c14" :question/math-academy-id "e-10050" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ |\n\nCompute $f_{X|Y}(2 \\,|\\, 2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function $f_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(2 \\,|\\, 2) = P(X = 2 \\,|\\, Y = 2).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X = 2 \\,|\\, Y = 2) = \\dfrac{P(X = 2, Y = 2)}{P(Y = 2)} = \\dfrac{f(2,2)}{f_Y(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(2) & =f(1,2)+f(2,2)+f(3,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(2,2) = \\dfrac{1}{6}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  $f_Y(2) = \\dfrac{7}{18}$ and $f(2,2) = \\dfrac{1}{6}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(2\\,|\\,2) & =P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{7}{18}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161680 :question/id #uuid "f1c9e136-57b3-59ae-90e0-b28921be44ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-10101" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n![](images/5a/5ae264514c53b97339bfa9969057ed9462f183fc8a77658bd0615f0af148a67c.svg)\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function $f_{X \\,|\\, Y} (x \\,|\\, y)$ for $0 \\leq             x \\leq             1, \\: 0 \\leq             y \\leq  1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+y-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2y+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for $f_Y(y)$ is the following:\n![](images/27/27ba59d670061d91b96f80ee5400824993311d35c9edf7a887957936d74ace8c.svg)\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{X \\,|\\, Y} (x \\,|\\, y) = \\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_Y (y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0 \\leq             x \\leq             1, \\: 0 \\leq             y \\leq  1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{\\left(\\frac{2y+1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+y)}{2y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, $f_{X \\,|\\, Y}(x \\,|\\, y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n![](images/24/242dc27ab006f389610d8f3bcd0297eed7d1809fb33b1b3b7c8b1f6b8dd532f7.svg)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161694 :question/id #uuid "3de4a4e5-824f-504b-ad7c-f088dd7569fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10315" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le x,\\,\\,0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}, & 0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=x\\big]$ for $0 <     x <     2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\big[ Y \\,|\\, X = x \\big] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y \\, f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, x) \\; \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\textrm E\\big[ Y \\,|\\, X = x \\big]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{x+y}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+y)}{3{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\leq y \\leq x,\\; 0 <              x <             2.$ Outside this domain, we have $f_{Y|X} (y \\,|\\, x) = 0.$ \n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\cdot \\,\\frac{2(x+y)}{3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}}[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5{x}^{3}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5{x}^{2}\\cdot x}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot x}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161708 :question/id #uuid "6562853a-696d-5cb5-a79d-76ff1a084d90" :question/math-academy-id "e-10384" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cf18d64c3f283c730e1d32ee81e3f193e615bb7b0b936dac009c46328826b3d.svg)\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\textrm {E}[X]= \\dfrac{3}{4},\\qquad\n\\textrm {E}[Y]= \\dfrac{5}{12}, \\qquad\n\\textrm {SD}[X]\\approx 0.1936, \\qquad\n\\textrm {SD}[Y]\\approx 0.2444,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] }{\\textrm {SD}[X] \\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}xy\\cdot 2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}(2{x}^{2}y+2x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{5}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{48}\\right)}{(0.1936)\\cdot (0.2444)} \\\\  & ≈0.44,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161796 :question/id #uuid "8be540db-a27c-537c-a804-35ed7cc45168" :question/math-academy-id "e-8244" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n![](images/a3/a3de8797a3b489ada861edbebb5de935dc5c840dafc509bf2b41ad093d7cc2b4.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x\\leq \\pi$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos x}{2} = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+k\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only $x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ lies within the interval $\\left[0, \\pi \\right].$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0, \\pi.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0 \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161846 :question/id #uuid "6276f196-80de-58b7-acb6-a715ee955f6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9073" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/50/501074b2c8aa951c9236dc628de2a564f31b21b5ac5b4dd34d7b1324d4c8a331.svg)\nIf $Y =1-3X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X,$ where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = 1-3X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $0 < x < 1,$ we have $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1 .$\n\n![](images/3b/3bb641b1920d0ded33e4ffa25f3e0af856550dd3820edf30416bd79c3a6da264.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(1-3X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-3X\\le y-1) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge \\frac{1-y}{3}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}\\left(\\frac{1-y}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1-y}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{1-y}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{1-y}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-y}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(1-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n![](images/b0/b00d02901c82c0544e8e576577832184919da2338cf05d2d8a3fa230d45993c4.svg)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161847 :question/id #uuid "b9f862ab-0544-503e-8627-3de73ec60594" :question/math-academy-id "e-9074" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/33/3357fbadc12363114c7274b17df9f8e992566d53f8233841783338687b88989e.svg)\nIf $Y = (2-X)^3,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X,$ where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = (2-X)^3$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0 \\lt x \\lt 2,$ we have $0 \\lt y \\lt 8 .$\n\n![](images/2c/2c07ffe5af282ba1689cb6f49201b979afb4186d0128dd6a97f446b7240d76d6.png)\n\n \n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0 \\lt y \\lt 8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P((2-X)^{3}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2-X\\le {y}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =P(-X\\le {y}^{1/3}-2) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge 2-{y}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(2-{y}^{1/3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $0 \\lt y \\lt 8,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\right)\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(2-{y}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(2-{y}^{1/3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(2-{y}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(2-(2-{y}^{1/3}))^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(2-2+{y}^{1/3})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}({y}^{1/3})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n![](images/d8/d858994c802b37d5867c0b8940848fec484bf42c48ecb525c5d518165ade60c6.svg)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161875 :question/id #uuid "1ebb1eb5-e7f2-557c-aafa-e11dcb2b04e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9207" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](images/6e/6efcc38c3e389b9f858b71d089077924d9780b4b3daf771c4682e88098393977.svg)\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\:  0\\lt x\\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},0\\lt y \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.$\n\n![](images/cc/ccb7cc52f8404dcca5d555d4fd81e6ae03e9689ccdd7c462fccbc2dc611db235.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}c\\sin (x+y) \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =c{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =c[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))] \\\\  & =c[(1-0)-(0-1)] \\\\  & =c[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n2c=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161888 :question/id #uuid "9fb6f57e-7fae-5e29-be45-5d1d198628f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9338" :question/problem "The salaries of a company's employees range from $\\[math]30\\,000$ to $\\[/math]90\\,000$ dollars. A company director wishes to carry out a hypothesis test with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The salaries are distributed uniformly.\n- $H_1:\\:$ The salaries are not distributed uniformly.\n\nA random sample of employees is taken, and the frequency distribution of their salaries is shown below. Calculate the expected frequency $E_i$ for each salary category.\n\n| Salary (thousands of $\\[math]$) | $30\\leq s_i < 42$ | $42\\leq s_i < 60$ | $60\\leq s_i < 80$ | $80\\leq s_i \\leq 90$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $12$ | $10$ | $6$ | $2$ |\n| Expected Frequency $(E_i)$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ be the salary of a randomly selected employee (in thousands of dollars). Under the null hypothesis, we have that $X\\sim U[30,90]$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(a\\leq X\\lt b)= \\dfrac{b-a}{90-30} = \\dfrac{b-a}{60}\n$$\n\nwhere $a \\lt b$ and $a,b\\in [30,90]$.\n\nThe sample size is\n\n$$\nN = 12+10+6+2 = 30\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each class of observations are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(30 \\le X < 42) \\\\\n&= 30 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{42 - 30}{60}\\right) \\\\\n&= 6 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(42 \\le X < 60) \\\\\n&= 30 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{60 - 42}{60}\\right) \\\\\n&= 9 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(60 \\le X < 80) \\\\\n&= 30 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{80 - 60}{60}\\right) \\\\\n&= 10 \\\\\nE_{4} &= N \\cdot P(80 \\le X \\le 90) \\\\\n&= 30 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{90 - 80}{60}\\right) \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n| Salary (thousands of $\\[math]$) | $30\\leq s_i < 42$ | $42\\leq s_i < 60$ | $60\\leq s_i < 80$ | $80\\leq s_i \\leq 90$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $12$ | $10$ | $6$ | $2$ |\n| Expected Frequency $(E_i)$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161889 :question/id #uuid "0349d317-e850-55d2-bec8-3b8fadc21180" :question/math-academy-id "e-9339" :question/problem "The salaries of a company's employees range from $\\[math]30\\,000$ to $\\[/math]90\\,000$ dollars. A random sample of employees is taken, and the frequency distribution of their salaries is shown below.\n\n| Salary (thousands of $\\[math]$) | $30\\leq y_i \\lt 40$ | $40\\leq y_i \\lt 60$ | $60\\leq y_i \\lt 80$ | $80\\leq y_i \\leq 90$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $\\,27\\,$ | $\\,49\\,$ | $\\,27\\,$ | $\\,17\\,$ |\n\nConduct a chi-squre goodness -of-fit test at the $5\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The salaries are distributed uniformly.\n- $H_1:\\:$ The salaries are not distributed uniformly.\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = p$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $\\,9.210\\,$ | $\\,5.991\\,$ | $\\,4.605\\,$ |\n| $3$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,13.277\\,$ | $\\,9.488\\,$ | $\\,7.779\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,15.086\\,$ | $\\,11.070\\,$ | $\\,9.236\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nLet $Y$ be the salary of a randomly selected employee (in thousands of dollars). Under the null hypothesis, we have that $Y\\sim U[30,90]$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(a\\leq Y\\lt b)= \\dfrac{b-a}{90-30} = \\dfrac{b-a}{60}\n$$\n\nwhere $a \\lt b$ and $a,b\\in [30,90]$.\n\nThe sample size is\n\n$$\nN = 27+49+27+17 = 120\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each class of observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(30 \\le Y < 40) \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{40 - 30}{60}\\right) \\\\\n&= 20 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(40 \\le Y < 60) \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{60 - 40}{60}\\right) \\\\\n&= 40 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(60 \\le Y < 80) \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{80 - 60}{60}\\right) \\\\\n&= 40 \\\\\nE_{4} &= N \\cdot P(80 \\le Y \\le 90) \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{90 - 80}{60}\\right) \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n| Salary (thousands of $\\[math]$) | $30\\leq y_i \\lt 40$ | $40\\leq y_i \\lt 60$ | $60\\leq y_i \\lt 80$ | $80\\leq y_i \\leq 90$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $\\,27\\,$ | $\\,49\\,$ | $\\,27\\,$ | $\\,17\\,$ |\n| $E_i$ | $\\,20\\,$ | $\\,40\\,$ | $\\,40\\,$ | $\\,20\\,$ |\n| $\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$ | $\\,2.45\\,$ | $\\,2.025\\,$ | $\\,4.225\\,$ | $\\,0.45\\,$ |\n\nWe compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 2.45 + 2.025 + 4.225 + 0.45 \\\\\n&= 9.15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is only one constraint on our data: the total number of observations must equal $N$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 4 - 1 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=3$ at a $5\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=7.815\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 7.815\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62ad82a0764f10c5ace9ae36e49c87c10121238f9b94db6062b6b6541352c128.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(9.15)$ lies in the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161892 :question/id #uuid "2b78e8fc-9d17-5a05-8adb-5e1e8920ddc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9351" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a continuous random variable with support $S=[0,120]$. A random sample is carried out, and the frequency distribution of the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Class | $0\\leq y_i < 30$ | $30\\leq y_i< 90$ | $90\\leq y_i \\leq 120$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $31$ | $42$ | $27$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness of fit test is conducted at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: Y\\sim U[0,120]$\n- $H_1:\\:Y\\not\\sim U[0,120]$\n\nCalculate the value of the test statistic for this hypothesis test." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nUnder the null hypothesis, we have that $Y\\sim U[0,120]$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(a\\leq Y\\lt b)= \\dfrac{b-a}{120-0} = \\dfrac{b-a}{120}\n$$\n\nwhere $a \\lt b$ and $a,b\\in [0,120]$.\n\nThe sample size is\n\n$$\nN = 31 + 42 + 27 = 100\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each class of observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(0 \\le Y < 30) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{30 - 0}{120}\\right) \\\\\n&= 25 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(30 \\le Y < 90) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{90 - 30}{120}\\right) \\\\\n&= 50 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(90 \\le Y < 120) \\\\\n&= 100 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{120 - 90}{120}\\right) \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n| Class | $0\\leq y_i < 30$ | $30\\leq y_i< 90$ | $90\\leq y_i \\leq 120$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $31$ | $42$ | $27$ |\n| $E_i$ | $25$ | $50$ | $25$ |\n| $\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$ | $1.44$ | $1.28$ | $0.16$ |\n\nWe compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 1.44 + 1.28 + 0.16 \\\\\n&= 2.88\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the test statistic is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.88}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7fa984-dcc6-51d3-a178-09d0e7e701a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551699} {:answer/id #uuid "39e8db46-65a2-5088-a0fc-7d008a801433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551803} {:answer/id #uuid "600d0ed3-d6f7-5bed-9243-c5cd652cb251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551897} {:answer/id #uuid "69593d18-dc90-550d-8f2b-fef2e3f9c883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551910} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7be294-62d9-5518-9eb0-0262367daae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-8y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69593d18-dc90-550d-8f2b-fef2e3f9c883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551910} :answer-field/id #uuid "64902e3c-53ad-5d5a-a122-1bd9b485f300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80402a14-4dfc-5a15-8269-ddd0f957785c" :question/math-academy-id "q-108501" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x},\\, & x>0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x>0,$ we have $y>0.$\n\n![](images/8b/8b2d1487d180db1df306cd8c83088ef0334d29769bd7ef94eed7f75d4e6cedf7.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y>0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{-4\\cdot y/2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b05969d-ca4f-52b6-bc88-130f089f4859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{Y}(t) = \\frac{1}{4}e^{-2t} + \\frac{3}{4}e^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158745} {:answer/id #uuid "ae662c1f-3a6d-5b6d-9467-1e33cbb0712f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{Y}(t) = \\frac{1}{4}e^{-4t} + \\frac{3}{4}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158746} {:answer/id #uuid "bc42fbc5-d5b1-5271-bcb3-4868dfd91e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{Y}(t) = \\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}e^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158747} {:answer/id #uuid "51dee472-9d88-551f-9585-0db3f33490d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{Y}(t) = \\frac{3}{4}e^{2t} + \\frac{1}{4}e^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158748} {:answer/id #uuid "52a11470-fac3-5e2c-bb33-b6624ea60ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{Y}(t) = \\frac{3}{4}e^{-4t} + \\frac{1}{4}e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158749}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ba01ceb3-df6d-5601-9922-ad8f03d39740" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158744}] :question/id #uuid "90aa1425-4503-5e16-b23c-dbd9a3c51caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-108874" :question/problem "You're given that if $X∼Bernoulli(p)$, the moment-generating function (MGF) of $X$ is\n$M_{X}(t) = 1 - p + pe^{t}, t ∈ R$.\n\nLet $Y = 2X - 4$, where $X∼Bernoulli\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$. What is the MGF of $Y$ for $t ∈ R$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df20dc58-5b67-5b4a-a4b8-eca19f0be704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158799} {:answer/id #uuid "1253e8d1-5192-596f-970c-1553a00ebc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158800} {:answer/id #uuid "2610e250-1e87-5e70-911e-ced87d38d5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158801} {:answer/id #uuid "322e03be-95c4-5fdf-9538-cfd90f4a2b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158802} {:answer/id #uuid "7d810ec3-fb2e-5820-ae2e-d55f7775e2d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2610e250-1e87-5e70-911e-ced87d38d5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158801} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdd49fee-8241-5a92-8763-eb72406aa943" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f809e4ca-96d1-5ff3-9f14-e230cdb13096" :question/math-academy-id "q-108945" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=7,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[5X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5X] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=7.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6)+{9}^{2}\\cdot f(9) \\\\  & =25\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)+36\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+81\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-49 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5X] & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =14\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af8dce06-ec2e-54f0-8068-081d29a00d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158805} {:answer/id #uuid "f65b10d3-6570-596d-aef7-36a7d9e90bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158806} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c1bb8e-9e55-5585-849e-6e82c2d6778c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158807} {:answer/id #uuid "2866549e-9133-5ffd-b73b-2bb7a74f8397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158808} {:answer/id #uuid "4319aba1-1a6d-55f0-8649-0a11c8e26ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2866549e-9133-5ffd-b73b-2bb7a74f8397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158808} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f5d0fa-e64f-5420-894c-9af20eb4994a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07dc96f1-44f9-56f8-847d-dfc0e5291318" :question/math-academy-id "q-108946" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=7,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[2-5X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-5$ and $b=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2-5X] & =(-5)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=7.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6)+{9}^{2}\\cdot f(9) \\\\  & =25\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)+36\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)+81\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-49 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2-5X] & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =14\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a22938f3-e3f3-5362-964f-f10b46383c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245760} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b3cf30-4246-5293-a6ba-401dfbcd91dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245786} {:answer/id #uuid "ab559822-e626-5ec7-9216-e68dd31ae519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245846} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d201b6-473a-581f-a380-899fc82c3d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246062} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb902c8-35bf-55d3-96c1-03b177b9d094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffb902c8-35bf-55d3-96c1-03b177b9d094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246546} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc701cce-934a-56e4-afcf-8dabca6a89d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79bec26d-9048-578e-8d3b-b8cd912ffc46" :question/math-academy-id "q-110368" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{x}^{2}y,\\, & {x}^{2}\\le y<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y<1\\}.$\n\n![](images/b9/b9cfac7f0f82088900ab5e603588a73d4932f08f592321c797a1fd94b8bafb4e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[c{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{7}}{14})]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4c}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4c}{21}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4607d8fe-1cdd-5306-938f-3172031dd831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476353} {:answer/id #uuid "5f9cf333-db05-520c-8de7-1f3f6733a1a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476418} {:answer/id #uuid "67819e46-288e-5f77-b645-210a90db3919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476436} {:answer/id #uuid "af323cc2-0a85-51fb-9281-8a9083c7e485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476592} {:answer/id #uuid "cef23f61-0eea-5b5e-a2f7-069305165076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67819e46-288e-5f77-b645-210a90db3919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476436} :answer-field/id #uuid "d563bd6b-11bb-52c8-afe9-6bc718772da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5f2de58-74ca-56e4-8d5c-d4fc6f4f531c" :question/math-academy-id "q-111898" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(X+Y)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},$ where $f(x)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/a4/a4f7e4e3f5e6a46fdc1b61a40986aec7b47fe75b37c8f042497aa0f3a0969f30.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(X+Y)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)]}_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8226456a-01f0-5577-ae2a-e36c51dc2d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159309} {:answer/id #uuid "97023f76-2165-5875-884b-00124cfc7eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159310} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6021b0-6cc8-5e87-80d1-4225584902b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159311} {:answer/id #uuid "32780e7c-aac0-5688-a09b-948445ba520b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159312} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0c73b3-1f00-5eb0-b4a8-0954c2dec4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97023f76-2165-5875-884b-00124cfc7eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159310} :answer-field/id #uuid "2914e011-6467-5eb9-b672-4230ceda87a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a22cb81e-00a5-5c3a-9983-3ce07b82cf44" :question/math-academy-id "q-137105" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $9a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 16 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}72 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}72 & -36 & 20 \\\\ -36 & 18 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}72 & -36 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n9a+\\frac{b}{c}=9\\cdot \\frac{5}{18}+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{1}=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dcc4c86-8874-58a9-ac30-b8fea76a7d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159465} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3d5dc9-0fd4-5e6b-94c2-978465a52970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159466} {:answer/id #uuid "b9353545-9e08-5666-a6b7-97b5a89449c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159467} {:answer/id #uuid "e51d7c3c-6f36-5161-a5be-051da9620ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159468} {:answer/id #uuid "61afbaa8-4c6f-50be-8b2e-786f372efc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159469}] :answer-field/id #uuid "147db7a1-8e41-55a8-82a0-9c06fdf5d804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159464} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c7ec135-93a1-53fb-9be9-e5178fa5fab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363323} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1de2e8-a448-5e66-944d-122e2c188b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363324} {:answer/id #uuid "4bed25d7-00fa-547f-a82d-e18995cf1214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363325} {:answer/id #uuid "7be8b03f-0d44-5529-9c7f-aeda802358b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363326} {:answer/id #uuid "f88fd16f-04d7-533e-b560-f1675f5e1366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f1de2e8-a448-5e66-944d-122e2c188b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363324} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7e74945-a9e0-5d8f-b2de-cafa74490fcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9686b88-3d11-53fe-b55c-13e35af5fe85" :question/math-academy-id "q-142906" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $\\frac{5}{18}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nCompute $P(2\\le Y\\le 6\\,|\\,X=2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\le Y\\le 6\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{P((2\\le Y\\le 6),X=2)}{P(X=2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=2,(2\\le Y\\le 6))}{P(X=2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=2,Y=2)+P(X=2,Y=4)+P(X=2,Y=6)}{P(X=2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2,2)+f(2,4)+f(2,6)}{{f}_{X}(2)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(2) & =f(2,0)+f(2,2)+f(2,4)+f(2,6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{18}+0+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(2,2)=\\frac{1}{18},f(2,4)=0$ and $f(2,6)=\\frac{1}{6}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting our values into our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le Y\\le 6\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)+f(2,4)+f(2,6)}{{f}_{X}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{18}+0+\\frac{1}{6})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a3ffab8-22b1-5ff6-b581-2df35360fc18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159549} {:answer/id #uuid "e2eb2f4e-7ab5-5535-849b-49596a5e1b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159550} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b0bd0d-58a5-5da2-a7d3-f2d3defec647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159551} {:answer/id #uuid "28844849-77a7-5df3-890d-de9c0191d526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159552} {:answer/id #uuid "7078d5e7-d2e8-5893-b018-fc36ef965370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2eb2f4e-7ab5-5535-849b-49596a5e1b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159550} :answer-field/id #uuid "72b97b25-0523-5c03-8564-83a07b086085" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11a32556-0b83-5605-b76d-705cb2b8ca2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143460" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,0<x<y,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{5}{8}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{5}{8}) & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{5}{8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{2x}{{\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{25}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{25}[{x}^{2}]_{1/4}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{25}(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{25}\\left(\\frac{3}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4b973f-cb9d-5e7e-8beb-4c556f2b875c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413400} {:answer/id #uuid "6a95ee3c-3035-5909-a68e-b2eac9893340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413402} {:answer/id #uuid "726964d4-b990-596e-9009-e83d6d208a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+3y)(y+1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413403} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e4876d-bca4-5991-b345-bf1e1f8f35e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(y+1)}{5(2x+3y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413406} {:answer/id #uuid "e77674d9-5e17-517e-8f5b-956421030e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b4b973f-cb9d-5e7e-8beb-4c556f2b875c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413400} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b4844fd-48c8-5187-86c9-fcbf91d0bc32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "228badef-897c-5f4a-80d9-699c2c689782" :question/math-academy-id "q-143634" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{4xy}{5}+\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{5}+\\frac{6x{y}^{2}}{5}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8y}{5}+\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{2y}{5}-\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{6y}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c1c0b73-5ac1-5777-bfdf-58a4281c67b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159627} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcad6a1-0066-5ef5-bcff-fc5fb045253b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{729}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159628} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a4ab47-6d4d-5808-9250-ca3607411754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159629} {:answer/id #uuid "47618d4a-1e91-5292-bb29-a4af40c4182f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159630} {:answer/id #uuid "50715a91-1def-5134-8929-3c112d077fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bcad6a1-0066-5ef5-bcff-fc5fb045253b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{729}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159628} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a3029e5-63e4-5b34-a3fc-488089fe2e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dae29a86-3127-5375-982e-23502adaa206" :question/math-academy-id "q-143870" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{3}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9{y}^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le {y}^{3},\\,0\\le y\\le 3; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=x{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{3},\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/68/68da52d856f882614241d72cff4dc3f5947cf16a6c9f58b0aea1c58d7eb375cc.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X{Y}^{3}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}x{y}^{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}x{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{9{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{7}\\text{ }\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{[\\frac{{y}^{8}}{8}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\left(\\frac{{3}^{8}}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{729}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246d1671-2920-5107-938a-d5818924161b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{2}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555072} {:answer/id #uuid "408fabb1-72b8-5f80-b166-4b2097274a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y, & 1<y<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555141} {:answer/id #uuid "643a424e-3a5b-5283-bde6-ee03fbed2dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555226} {:answer/id #uuid "858132b6-0402-5b5e-b535-8e91c02ecb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{y-2}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555307} {:answer/id #uuid "94dcdc7d-dc32-5d1c-b053-4b6089835648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{e-1}, & 1<y<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "643a424e-3a5b-5283-bde6-ee03fbed2dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555226} :answer-field/id #uuid "02e0b4df-b03a-5331-8fad-a3680e2a7c52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f7b4c3d-9f53-5a0a-9f65-57f007102f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-143899" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}, & 1<x<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{1}{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $1<x<e,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=e,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=1,\\,u(e)={e}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cfb93cf-bd42-5bbd-b926-c729076a7176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528595} {:answer/id #uuid "9d05d328-ed87-5676-8000-bf330c49b276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528676} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e11f88-ae8b-5b32-bcc7-944cd00e9429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528853} {:answer/id #uuid "f86d5c54-dcc9-590b-831d-717992f4b407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528917} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4e3252-f147-5beb-9e9a-fc41e8f66725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cfb93cf-bd42-5bbd-b926-c729076a7176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528595} :answer-field/id #uuid "10b50755-e223-5dd6-8034-940c39f83d0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d9910fd-054f-5969-9b22-8e682fab832a" :question/math-academy-id "q-145622" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}, & {x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1,\\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(1-{x}^{2}), & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ for $0<x<1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{\\frac{3}{2}(1-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1,\\,0<x<1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}y\\cdot \\,\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{{x}^{2}}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{1-{x}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{(1-{x}^{2})}(1+{x}^{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b722803a-370b-5cc6-8dec-952c663984fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$sup(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160503} {:answer/id #uuid "128ee1a2-ad98-5075-846f-b8cbe5627a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487604} {:answer/id #uuid "7d320ab4-e7ea-5b1a-9b1e-fcd8c46760b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=1\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487808} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2c2649-8169-5a93-bf6e-55c2f61519ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488011} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb9e03b-cb3d-5a8d-9ae0-14d4a6eba10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=1\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "128ee1a2-ad98-5075-846f-b8cbe5627a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487604} :answer-field/id #uuid "fce77323-ea42-5087-8e83-d953a7ffc6f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4afd4c3a-6ec4-5cdc-b8ec-af8373f2b829" :question/math-academy-id "q-253840" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{1+3{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=1+3{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/19/198c3d6737439fcfa204926394b317aae0026502b5eaacb9e2f0429941f23db3.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $1.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{1}=1+3{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{1}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{1}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{7}{4}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\in A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8957db0d-2788-5a97-accb-1b58cd4b66b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160516} {:answer/id #uuid "a0686524-8356-572f-894e-8262c0dc086e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160517} {:answer/id #uuid "ea33f1a2-ddc4-51ac-8801-f93a60815cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160518} {:answer/id #uuid "fdaa8aa6-591e-56f4-8217-28fd521d6ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160519} {:answer/id #uuid "351d15ae-b433-53db-902d-607a857741c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160520}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c6963e2d-6e35-58d7-9a45-0bb0c05e03ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06e0dd97-a345-54a7-b1bc-dd6d9b0e3b1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-253887" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼Exp\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$, calculate $P(X > 3 \\mid X > 1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e15f8108-ad7e-5270-83ff-31eea9376ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161055} {:answer/id #uuid "0c8be9a6-2d3e-5a69-8273-a4cdfdceab06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161056} {:answer/id #uuid "5917c00d-f63b-50f7-bad1-1d661e341857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{e}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161057} {:answer/id #uuid "612c2860-028a-5d77-b250-72f3583ca108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161058} {:answer/id #uuid "65067eb9-911a-564e-8bfb-4686ee35cb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161059}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1b87eb-44d2-55e5-b7df-a536135fbe45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161054}] :question/id #uuid "11b61c93-9964-5c89-b85a-5bd388eb8259" :question/math-academy-id "q-94433" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $Y$ if the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$f ( y ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}5 {e}^{- 5 y} , \\hspace{1em} & y \\ge  0 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03758742-4e66-5fc5-b8d2-c064330220a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481775} {:answer/id #uuid "28f8e7b0-163f-5d34-82c2-5019a5d8f120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481879} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcdd795-b4e2-5697-a229-4d5c328622b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481962} {:answer/id #uuid "8a57ef84-dc66-5261-bb34-5e50fd7f3719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482098} {:answer/id #uuid "ee44477a-a24b-5936-8441-328751567d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dcdd795-b4e2-5697-a229-4d5c328622b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481962} :answer-field/id #uuid "43bc050a-7f4d-5ec6-bef2-a525c0cde7b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e1b7e9e-2574-5316-a0b8-5178c531c66d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94576" :question/problem "Find the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-{q}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {e}^{1-{q}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-{q}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\ {q}_{1} & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-{q}_{3}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {e}^{1-{q}_{3}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-{q}_{3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\ {q}_{3} & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a3ec05-1796-5998-8e30-938d46d50a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481881} {:answer/id #uuid "39494504-cec8-5020-9908-1c5cb6914a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481917} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd3667f-22b1-5593-a711-58ec7a41392e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481929} {:answer/id #uuid "a0567618-3fff-5bf8-8a81-37e04f3d33bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482155} {:answer/id #uuid "fc18e48a-eca0-55b3-8692-d7cfbe38ebd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc18e48a-eca0-55b3-8692-d7cfbe38ebd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482372} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ef4dfd0-f0cc-51fe-86d6-3a8aa37b7302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bfa7b9d-151d-5ab9-bbf5-f49790dd348d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94701" :question/problem "Find the $30$th percentile of the random variable $Y$ if the probability distribution function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $Y$ is the number ${y}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le {y}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $30$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.3.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le {y}_{0.3}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{{y}_{0.3}}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-y}{|}_{0}^{{y}_{0.3}} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-{y}_{0.3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $30$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-{y}_{0.3}} & =0.3 \\\\ {e}^{-{y}_{0.3}} & =0.7 \\\\ {e}^{-{y}_{0.3}} & =\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ -{y}_{0.3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{0.3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{0.3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2e3373e-36b0-5d84-9e12-7c37479aece9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6824" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161223} {:answer/id #uuid "b03364e5-83ca-5894-afee-032021fcded9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6616" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161224} {:answer/id #uuid "37ac3a4e-7d90-5d40-9e02-6baf810661ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7817" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161225} {:answer/id #uuid "5029c6a1-697f-5d64-8192-e3cfb3d508d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7165" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161226} {:answer/id #uuid "724076df-74b2-53c6-a073-49cc49606f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7354" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161227}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b24b8e5a-d148-5ec4-90e5-47c2d38ff86a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00460ff6-9869-572e-a081-8348a6b5f98e" :question/